  <text id="shot-label" x="70" y="146" width="712" height="14" font="micro" size="11" color="muted" align="center" wrap="false">Real web screenshot • browser-rendered parcel map with seeded Nigerian parcel coordinates and live map tiles</text>
  <shape id="right-card" type="rect" x="826" y="138" width="396" height="424" fill="mist" stroke="hairline" stroke-width="1"/>
  <text id="right-title" x="850" y="164" width="348" height="26" font="body" size="20" color="ink"><p><strong>Why this matters in Nigeria</strong></p></text>
  <text id="right-copy" x="850" y="204" width="348" height="314" font="body" size="17" color="ink" line-height="1.34">
    <p>The platform now shows a real browser-rendered map in the web product, centered on a seeded parcel in Epe, Lagos. This closes the evidence gap between parcel metadata and visible map context.</p>
    <p space-before="14"><strong>Operational value includes:</strong></p>
    <p space-before="10">• Faster review of parcel location and surrounding context.</p>
    <p>• Better alignment between land records, field actions, and approval decisions.</p>
    <p>• Stronger support for right-of-way, housing, infrastructure, mining, and extractives workflows where geography drives risk.</p>
    <p space-before="14">The web map strengthens desk-based review, while the native mobile experience still supports richer in-field geospatial continuity where device workflows are required.</p>
  </text>
